Choose the rank of the triple in 13 ways and its three cards in $\binom{4}{3}=4$ ways. Choose the rank of the pair in 12 ways and its two cards in $\binom{4}{2}=6$ ways. This counts each full house once because the triple and pair have distinct roles, giving $13\cdot4\cdot12\cdot6=3744$ full houses.
There are $\binom{52}{5}=52\cdot51\cdot50\cdot49\cdot48/120=2598960$ equally likely hands. The probability is $3744/2598960=6/4165$, dividing numerator and denominator by 624.
Final answer: \frac{6}{4165}
